<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mid-latitude synoptic scale analysis</title>

  <p>
    Characteristic values for synoptic scale motion in the <term>middle latitudes</term> (30°<ndash />60° latitude),
    which are based on observations of mid-latitude synoptic scale phenomena,
    are shown in <xref ref="table-synoptic-scales" text="type-global" /> below.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-synoptic-scales">
    <title>
        Characteristic scales for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions,
        adapted from Martin (2006) and Holton and Hakim (2013)
    </title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell>Quantity</cell>
            <cell>Symbol</cell>
            <cell>Characteristic scale</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Horizontal length</cell>
            <cell><m>L</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^6 \, \text{m}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Vertical depth</cell>
            <cell><m>H</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^4 \, \text{m}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Horizontal velocity</cell>
            <cell><m>U</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Vertical velocity</cell>
            <cell><m>W</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-2} \, \text{m} \,\text{s}^{-1}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>(Advective) time</cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^5 \, \text{s}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Horizontal pressure fluctuation</cell>
            <cell><m>\delta p</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^3 \, \text{Pa}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Normalized horizontal pressure fluctuation</cell>
            <cell><m>\sfrac{\delta p}{\rho}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^3 \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{s}^{-2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Vertical pressure fluctuation</cell>
            <cell><m>P_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^5 \, \text{Pa}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Density</cell>
            <cell><m>\rho_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1 \, \text{kg} \, \text{m}^{-3}</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Furthermore,
    for synoptic scale motion in the middle latitudes,
    constants that appear in the equations of motion scale
    according to <xref ref="table-synoptic-scales-constants" text="type-global" /> below.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-synoptic-scales-constants">
    <title>
        Characteristic scales for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions,
        adapted from Martin (2006) and Holton and Hakim (2013)
    </title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell>Constant</cell>
            <cell>Symbol</cell>
            <cell>Characteristic scale</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Earth's mean radius</cell>
            <cell><m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^7 \, \text{m}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Gravity acceleration</cell>
            <cell><m>g_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Kinematic viscosity</cell>
            <cell><m>\nu_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-5} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{s}^{-1}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Latitude</cell>
            <cell><m>\phi_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>45^{\circ}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Coriolis parameter</cell>
            <cell><m>f_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-4} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Note that the characteristic horizontal length scale <m>L</m> is 100 times larger than the characteristic vertical depth scale <m>H</m>.
    This is a stand-out feature of synoptic scale weather phenomena:
    These phenomena are very large horizontally but are confined to the troposphere vertically.
    As we will see below,
    these scales lead to interesting simplifications of the equations of motion.
  </p>

  <p>
    Application of these characteristic scales to the horizontal equations of motion
    (i.e., the zonal <m>x</m> and meridional <m>y</m> component equations of motion)
    is shown in <xref ref="table-horizontal-equations-of-motion-scaled" text="type-global" /> below,
    and application of these characteristic scales to the vertical equation of motion
    (i.e., the vertical <m>z</m> component equation of motion)
    is shown in <xref ref="table-vertical-equation-of-motion-scaled" text="type-global" /> below.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-horizontal-equations-of-motion-scaled">
    <title>
        Characteristic scales for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions for terms of the horizontal equations of motion,
        adapted from Martin (2006) Table 3.1 and Holton and Hakim (2013) Table 2.1
    </title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{Du}{Dt}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2 \Omega v \sin{\phi}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-2 \Omega w \cos{\phi}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{uw}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{uv \tan{\phi}}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{\rho} \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial x}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\nu \nabla^2 u</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>y</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{Dv}{Dt}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-2 \Omega u \sin{\phi}</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{vw}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{u^2 \tan{\phi}}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{\rho} \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial y}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\nu \nabla^2 v</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Scales</cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{U^2}{L}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f_0 U</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f_0 W</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{UW}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{U^2 \tan{\phi_0}}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{\delta p}{\rho L}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\nu_0 \dfrac{U}{H^2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Magnitude (<m>\text{m} \, \text{ s}^{-2}</m>)</cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-8}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-5}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-12}</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <table xml:id="table-vertical-equation-of-motion-scaled">
    <title>
        Characteristic scales for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions for terms of the vertical equation of motion,
        adapted from Martin (2006) Table 3.2 and Holton and Hakim (2013) Table 2.2
    </title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>z</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{Dw}{Dt}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>2 \Omega u \cos{\phi}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{u^2 + v^2}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{\rho} \dfrac{\partial p}{\partial z}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>g</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\nu \nabla^2 w</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Scales</cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{UW}{L}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f_0 U</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{U^2}{a}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\dfrac{P_0}{\rho_0 H}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>g_0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\nu_0 \dfrac{W}{H^2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Magnitude (<m>\text{m} \, \text{ s}^{-2}</m>)</cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-7}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-5}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10</m></cell>
            <cell><m>10^{-15}</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    As discussed in the next sub-sections,
    scale analysis reveals that,
    to a first-order approximation,
    mid-latitude synoptic scale motions are in geostrophic and hydrostatic balance.
  </p>

  <xi:include href="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-geostrophic-balance.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-rossby-number.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-hydrostatic-balance.ptx" />
</section>